# Chapter 11 · Case Study II: Malala Yousafzai

> "**Critically examine and evaluate Malala Yousafzai's achievements and activism for promoting girls' education in local and global contexts**" (==2025==). MCQs asked her year of birth (**1997**, 2016) and the cause she works for internationally (**education**, 2019).

## 11.1  Background

**Malala Yousafzai** was born on ==12 July 1997== in **Mingora**, in the **Swat** valley of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa. Her father, **Ziauddin Yousafzai**, a teacher and education activist, ran a chain of schools, the **Khushal Public School**. Unusually for the region, he celebrated his daughter's birth and encouraged her to speak her mind. She was named after **Malalai of Maiwand**, a Pashtun heroine.

## 11.2  Swat under the Taliban

From ==2007==, the **Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan** (TTP), led in Swat by **Maulana Fazlullah**, gained control of much of the valley. Using an illegal FM radio station, they announced bans on music, television and women's public life, and in ==December 2008== ordered that **girls' schools close** from mid-January 2009. They blew up or burned more than **100** girls' schools. A military operation (==2009==) later drove them out.

## 11.3  The activist emerges

::: fig timeline | Malala's journey.
@layout vertical
12 July 1997: born in Mingora, Swat
January 2009: BBC Urdu diary under the pen name "Gul Makai"
2009: New York Times documentary "Class Dismissed"
2011: Pakistan's first National Youth Peace Prize; nominated for the International Children's Peace Prize
9 October 2012: shot in the head by a Taliban gunman on her school bus
12 July 2013: speech at the United Nations — "Malala Day"
2013: I Am Malala published; Malala Fund co-founded with her father; Sakharov Prize
10 December 2014: Nobel Peace Prize, shared with Kailash Satyarthi — youngest laureate, at 17
2017: UN Messenger of Peace; begins study at Oxford
2018: first return visit to Pakistan
2020: graduates in Philosophy, Politics and Economics from Oxford
January 2025: speaks at the Girls' Education in Muslim Communities conference in Islamabad
:::

- **The BBC diary (2009):** at the age of **11**, writing under the pen name "**Gul Makai**" (cornflower), she wrote a diary for **BBC Urdu** about life under the Taliban and the fear of losing her schooling.
- **Media voice:** she appeared in a *New York Times* documentary and spoke on television about girls' right to education.
- **Recognition:** she received Pakistan's first **National Youth Peace Prize** (==2011==), later named the **National Malala Peace Prize**.

## 11.4  The attack — 9 October 2012

On ==9 October 2012==, a Taliban gunman boarded her **school bus** in Mingora, asked "Who is Malala?", and shot her in the **head**; two other girls, **Shazia Ramzan** and **Kainat Riaz**, were also wounded. The TTP claimed responsibility, calling her a symbol of "infidelity" and "obscenity". She was treated in Peshawar and Rawalpindi and flown to the **Queen Elizabeth Hospital, Birmingham**, in the United Kingdom, where she recovered.

The attack provoked worldwide condemnation. In Pakistan, there were vigils and rallies; politicians and religious leaders condemned it (though some hedged); the government announced rewards for the attackers.

## 11.5  Activism on a global stage

### (1) The UN speech (2013)

On her **16th birthday**, ==12 July 2013== — declared "**Malala Day**" by the UN — she addressed the **UN Youth Assembly** in New York: "**One child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world.**" She called on world leaders to provide free, compulsory education for every child.

::: quote Malala Yousafzai | United Nations, 12 July 2013
"Let us pick up our books and our pens. They are our most powerful weapons. One child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world. Education is the only solution."
-> The most quoted line of her activism.
:::

### (2) Malala Fund (2013)

With her father she co-founded the **Malala Fund**, which invests in local education activists ("**Education Champions**", through the **Gulmakai Network**) and advocates for policy change in countries where most girls miss secondary school — including **Pakistan**, **Afghanistan**, **Nigeria**, **India**, **Brazil**, **Ethiopia** and **Lebanon** (for Syrian refugees). By ==2025== it reported investing about **14.6 million dollars** in Pakistan since 2013.

### (3) Books

***I Am Malala*** (==2013==, with Christina Lamb) became a global best-seller; she also wrote ***We Are Displaced*** (==2019==) about refugee girls, a children's book (*Malala's Magic Pencil*, ==2017==) and a memoir, *Finding My Way* (==2025==).

### (4) The Nobel Peace Prize (2014)

On ==10 December 2014==, she shared the **Nobel Peace Prize** with the Indian child-rights activist **Kailash Satyarthi**, "for their struggle against the suppression of children and young people and for the right of all children to education". At **17**, she became the **youngest Nobel laureate** ever. She used the prize money to fund a school for girls in her ancestral district of **Shangla**.

### (5) Other honours

The **Sakharov Prize** of the European Parliament (==2013==), honorary **Canadian citizenship** (announced ==2014==, conferred 2017), **UN Messenger of Peace** (==2017==), and many others.

## 11.6  Local activism and impact in Pakistan

- **Shangla school:** a school for girls in Shangla, funded by her Nobel prize money.
- **Malala Fund in Pakistan:** grants to local organisations working on girls' secondary education, advocacy for higher education budgets, and campaigns on the effects of the 2022 floods on girls' schooling.
- **Policy voice:** she has pressed Pakistan to spend more on education and to enforce **Article 25A** (free, compulsory education to age 16).
- **Islamabad conference (2025):** in ==January 2025== she spoke at the **Muslim World League**'s "**Girls' Education in Muslim Communities**" conference in Islamabad, urging Muslim leaders to stand with **Afghan girls** and to recognise "**gender apartheid**" as a crime; the conference issued an **Islamabad Declaration** on girls' education.
- **Symbolic impact:** she showed that a girl from a conservative region could become a global leader, and that education is a right worth defending.

## 11.7  Global impact

- **Agenda-setting:** she helped keep **girls' education** on the international agenda — SDG 4 (==2015==), and campaigns for 12 years of free, safe, quality education.
- **Afghanistan:** since the Taliban's return in ==2021== and their ban on girls' secondary and higher education, she has been a leading voice calling for "**gender apartheid**" to be recognised in international law.
- **Refugees and conflict:** advocacy for girls displaced by conflict in Syria, Iraq and elsewhere.

## 11.8  Criticism and controversy in Pakistan

Malala is admired worldwide but **divisive** at home.

1. **"Western agent" claims:** some accuse her of serving a Western agenda and of shaming Pakistan abroad.
2. **Conspiracy theories:** some claimed the attack was staged — despite overwhelming evidence.
3. **Her book:** in ==2014== the **All Pakistan Private Schools Federation** observed an "**I am not Malala**" day and banned her book from its schools, objecting to passages on religion and Pakistan.
4. **Selective attention:** critics ask why the world honoured her while ignoring other victims — such as children killed in drone strikes.
5. **Limited local presence:** she lives abroad for security reasons, and some argue her impact on the ground is limited.

::: balance | Evaluating Malala's achievements (2025)
- **Achievements:** a global symbol of girls' education; the youngest Nobel laureate; the Malala Fund's investment in local activists in Pakistan and elsewhere; a leading voice on Afghan girls; inspiration for millions of girls.
- **Limits and criticisms:** Pakistan still has more than **25 million** out-of-school children, most of them girls; she lives abroad; she is a polarising figure at home, which may limit her local influence; symbolic impact is hard to measure.
-> **Line to take:** Malala's greatest achievement is **global agenda-setting and symbolic power**; her local impact is real but modest compared with the scale of Pakistan's education crisis. Her story shows both the power of individual courage and the need for **state action** — spending, schools and security — that no individual can replace.
:::

::: eye | The model structure for 2025
1. Introduction: who she is; the argument. 2. Background: Swat, her father, the Taliban ban. 3. Early activism: BBC diary, media. 4. The attack (9 October 2012). 5. Global activism: UN speech, Malala Fund, books, Nobel 2014. 6. Local activism: Shangla school, grants, budget advocacy, Islamabad 2025. 7. Global context: SDG 4, Afghanistan, refugees. 8. Critical evaluation: criticisms ("I am not Malala"), limits (25 million out of school). 9. Conclusion. Draw the timeline.
:::
